## [GAP ANALYSIS] Unsendable reminders skipped before the Twilio call never increment retryCount

**Severity:** MEDIUM — the "retry forever until the date is reached" behaviour the PR describes remains for reminders with no phone number / no message.

### The Problem

`retryCount` is only incremented in two places: the `else` of `if (scheduledSMS)` (line 178) and the `catch` (line 189). Two earlier exits bypass both:

```ts
if (!reminder.workflowStep || !reminder.booking) {
  continue;                                   // line 68-70: no increment
}
...
if (message?.length && message?.length > 0 && sendTo) {   // line 158
  ...
}                                             // no else: no increment
```

`sendTo` is `reminder.booking?.smsReminderNumber` for `SMS_ATTENDEE`, which is nullable, and `message` is `null` when the step has no `reminderBody` and a non-`REMINDER` template.

### Theory / Proof

The `findMany` at line 48 selects every `scheduled: false` SMS reminder due in the next 7 days. A reminder whose booking has no `smsReminderNumber` falls through line 158 silently, keeps `retryCount = 0`, stays `scheduled: false`, and is picked up again on the next run (the GitHub Actions cron runs `*/15 * * * *`, `.github/workflows/cron-scheduleSMSReminders.yml`). It is only removed by the `scheduledDate <= now` branch — i.e. the pre-PR behaviour.

### What happens today vs. what will break

Reminders that reach Twilio and fail are now dropped after two failures. Reminders that are structurally unsendable (the most permanent failure class) are not, and continue to be re-fetched with their full `select` (booking, attendees, eventType, workflowStep) up to 7 days x 96 runs/day.

### Research

Retry-budget patterns (e.g. BullMQ `attempts`, SQS `maxReceiveCount`) count every processing attempt that does not succeed, not only those that throw.

### Recommendation

Count every non-success outcome:

```ts
if (message?.length && sendTo) {
  const scheduledSMS = await twilio.scheduleSMS(...);
  if (scheduledSMS) { /* mark scheduled */ continue; }
}
await prisma.workflowReminder.update({
  where: { id: reminder.id },
  data: { retryCount: { increment: 1 } },
});
```

and do the same (or delete directly) for the `!workflowStep || !booking` case at line 68.

## [ISSUE] `retryCount > 1` delete branch is not scoped to SMS reminders

**Severity:** MEDIUM — latent cross-method data deletion plus an unindexed predicate on every cron run.

### The Problem

```ts
await prisma.workflowReminder.deleteMany({
  where: {
    OR: [
      { method: WorkflowMethods.SMS, scheduledDate: { lte: ... } },
      { retryCount: { gt: 1 } },          // <- any method
    ],
  },
});
```

The first branch is scoped to `method: SMS`; the second is not. `retryCount` was added as a generic column on `WorkflowReminder` (`schema.prisma`), which also stores `EMAIL` and `WHATSAPP` reminders handled by `scheduleEmailReminders.ts` and `scheduleWhatsappReminders.ts`.

### Theory / Proof

Prisma compiles `OR: [A, B]` to `WHERE (A) OR (B)`; `B` alone matches any row with `retryCount > 1` regardless of `method`. Today only this file writes `retryCount`, so nothing else is deleted yet — but the moment the WhatsApp or email cron adopts the same column (the obvious next step, since `scheduleWhatsappReminders.ts` is a near copy of this file), the SMS cron will delete those reminders on its own schedule, before their owning cron decides to.

Indexing: the only composite index is `@@index([method, scheduled, scheduledDate])`. Branch B has no indexed column, so Postgres cannot build a BitmapOr and must sequentially scan `WorkflowReminder` every 15 minutes.

### What happens today vs. what will break

Works today only because no other code sets `retryCount`. Breaks silently when another method starts using it.

### Recommendation

```ts
await prisma.workflowReminder.deleteMany({
  where: {
    method: WorkflowMethods.SMS,
    OR: [
      { scheduledDate: { lte: dayjs().toISOString() } },
      { retryCount: { gt: 1 } },
    ],
  },
});
```

This also lets the planner use the `method`-prefixed index for both branches.

## [ISSUE] Transient failures permanently delete reminders after ~30 minutes

**Severity:** MEDIUM — a short Twilio/network incident or a temporary team SMS lock silently drops all pending reminders.

### The Problem

Any exception and any falsy return from `twilio.scheduleSMS` bumps `retryCount`; on the next-but-one run `retryCount > 1` deletes the row:

```ts
} catch (error) {
  await prisma.workflowReminder.update({ ..., data: { retryCount: reminder.retryCount + 1 } });
  console.log(`Error scheduling SMS with error ${error}`);
}
```

### Theory / Proof

- The cron runs every 15 minutes (`.github/workflows/cron-scheduleSMSReminders.yml`: `*/15 * * * *`). Run N fails -> 1, run N+1 fails -> 2, run N+2 deletes. So two consecutive failed runs are enough.
- `twilio.scheduleSMS` (`lib/reminders/providers/twilioProvider.ts:90-95`) returns `undefined` when `isLockedForSMSSending` is true. Lock state is reversible by admins (`SMSLockState`), so a team that is locked and later unlocked loses every reminder that came due for scheduling in between.
- Twilio 5xx / timeouts / missing credentials during a deploy throw and land in the `catch`, indistinguishable from a 400 invalid-number error.

The cron fetches all unscheduled reminders due within 7 days, so one bad window affects every such reminder at once, and deletion is irreversible (no `cancelled` flag, no log of which rows were dropped).

### What happens today vs. what will break

Before the PR, transient failures self-healed on the next run. After it, a 30-minute Twilio incident removes up to a week's worth of reminders with only a `console.log`.

### Research

Twilio documents 4xx error codes such as 21211 (invalid 'To' number) and 21614 (not a mobile number) as permanent; 5xx and 20429 (too many requests) are retryable. Standard practice (e.g. AWS SDK retry classification, Stripe idempotent retries) is to only exhaust a retry budget on non-retryable errors.

### Recommendation

Only count permanent failures, and do not count the lock case:

```ts
} catch (error) {
  const status = (error as { status?: number }).status;
  if (status && status >= 400 && status < 500 && status !== 429) {
    await prisma.workflowReminder.update({
      where: { id: reminder.id },
      data: { retryCount: { increment: 1 } },
    });
  }
  console.log(`Error scheduling SMS for reminder ${reminder.id}: ${error}`);
}
```

and have `scheduleSMS` distinguish "locked" from "failed" instead of returning `undefined` for both.

## [ISSUE] Unguarded update in `catch` can abort the whole cron run

**Severity:** LOW — one failing row can stop every later reminder from being scheduled in that run.

### The Problem

```ts
} catch (error) {
  await prisma.workflowReminder.update({
    where: { id: reminder.id },
    data: { retryCount: reminder.retryCount + 1 },
  });
  console.log(...);
}
```

Previously the `catch` only logged, so the loop was guaranteed to continue. Now it performs a DB write that can itself throw, and nothing catches it.

### Theory / Proof

`prisma.x.update` throws `PrismaClientKnownRequestError` `P2025` when the `where` matches no row. The loop awaits Twilio sequentially for every reminder, so a run can take a long time; meanwhile booking cancellation / rescheduling deletes `WorkflowReminder` rows (`deleteScheduledSMSReminder`, `deleteMany` in the handlers). If the reminder whose Twilio call just failed has been deleted, the update throws, the exception leaves the `for` loop, `defaultHandler` returns 500, and every subsequent reminder is not processed until the next run. The same applies to a transient DB error.

The value is also written as a read-modify-write (`reminder.retryCount + 1` from the earlier `findMany`) rather than an atomic increment, so two overlapping cron invocations would both write the same value.

### Recommendation

```ts
} catch (error) {
  console.log(`Error scheduling SMS with error ${error}`);
  await prisma.workflowReminder
    .updateMany({ where: { id: reminder.id }, data: { retryCount: { increment: 1 } } })
    .catch((e) => console.log(`Failed to bump retryCount for ${reminder.id}: ${e}`));
}
```

`updateMany` does not throw on zero matches, and `{ increment: 1 }` is atomic.
